<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yushu Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yushu is a Tibetan autonomous prefecture in southern Qinghai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, covering high grasslands around the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> rivers. Historically part of the Tibetan region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>, it was the heartland of the kingdom of Nangchen and other Tibetan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. Its seat, Gyêgu, was largely destroyed by an earthquake in 2010 and has since been rebuilt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yushu-tibetan-autonomous-prefecture.jpg" alt="Yushu Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lincun · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qinghai_subdivisions_-_Yushu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yushu Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture covers 188,794 square kilometres in the south-west of Qinghai Province and is largely inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>. It is also transliterated Yüxü or Yulshul. Its seat is the town of Gyêgu in Yushu County, the place of the old Tibetan trade mart of Jyekundo, and the official source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> lies within it. Historically the area belongs to the cultural realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><p>Almost all of the prefecture lies in the uppermost basins of three of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>'s great rivers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, and a large part of it forms the Sanjiangyuan National Nature Reserve, which protects their headwaters. Most people live in the south-east, in the valleys of the upper Yangtze, known in Chinese as the Tongtian River and in Tibetan as Drichu, and of the Mekong, the Dzachu. The highlands and the west have very little population. Elevations are above 3,600 metres, with long, cold winters and short, rainy summers, and most rain falls from June to September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area is rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> monasteries. As part of the former kingdom of Nangchen, it was for most of its history not under the domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>'s Gelugpa order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, and this different balance of power allowed the older Tibetan Buddhist orders to prevail. Of 195 lamaseries before 1958 only 23 belonged to the Gelugpa. More than 100 followed the Kagyupa schools, some of whose sub-sects are found only in this part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The Sakyapa had 32 monasteries, many of them among the most significant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>, and the Nyingmapa had about as many. The Bönpo had only one, shared with the Nyingmapa.</p><p>Before collectivisation in 1958 the monastic population of the present prefecture exceeded 25,000 monks and nuns, among them about 300 incarnate lamas. On average three to five per cent of the people were monastic, and in Nangchen county the share was between 12 and 20 per cent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture is divided into five counties and one county-level city. Its produce includes wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and black highland barley. The eastern part is served by China National Highway 214 and by Yushu Batang Airport, which opened in 2009, and in 2017 the G0613 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xining">Xining</a>–Lijiang Expressway was completed. On 14 April 2010 an earthquake struck the prefecture at 07:49 local time, with a magnitude of 6.9 according to the USGS and EMSC and 7.1 according to Xinhua.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yushu Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nangchen, in today's <b>Yushu</b> region of southern Qinghai, emerged as a principality under a hereditary royal family in the Mongol period.</blockquote><a href="/state/nangchen">Nangchen</a></li></ul></section>
